# Planning Before Coding

## Objective

For any non-trivial task, form and (when useful) state a short plan before generating code, to avoid rework and half-finished implementations.

## Core Principles

- Identify affected files/modules before editing anything.
- Break multi-step tasks into an explicit ordered plan.
- Decide the data flow and interfaces first, implementation details second.
- Re-check the plan against existing code conventions before executing it.

## Requirements

- For tasks touching more than one file, list the files and the change to each before editing.
- For new features, state the approach in 2-5 bullet points before generating code.
- Identify edge cases and failure modes as part of the plan, not as an afterthought.
- If the plan changes mid-implementation, say so explicitly rather than silently deviating.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Jumping straight to code on ambiguous, multi-file, or architecture-affecting requests.
- Writing a plan that is immediately abandoned once code generation starts.
- Treating 'planning' as decorative text rather than something the implementation follows.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ A plan existed for any change spanning more than one file or introducing new structure.
✓ The implementation actually follows the stated plan.
✓ Edge cases identified in planning are handled in the code.

## Golden Rule

Time spent planning a multi-step change is repaid many times over in avoided rework.
